tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: From motte-and-bailey to the concentric castle", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Motte with timber tower, ditch, palisaded bailey with hall and chapel.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Norman and Crusader Europe, 11th-13th centuries", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "The Normans threw up earth mounds (mottes) topped by timber towers inside a palisaded yard", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "(bailey) in weeks; stone keeps followed, then curtain walls with round towers, and finally", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "concentric rings of walls each overlooking the next, learned partly from Byzantine and", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Islamic fortresses in the Levant.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Height gives the defender range and the attacker nothing; round towers have no corners for", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "mining and no blind spots. Arrow slits, machicolations (holes in the parapet floor),", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "murder holes over gates and a moat multiply the garrison's effect. Masyaf, Alamut and Krak", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "all sit on crags that remove the need for a moat.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "See it in context: /function ac:holy_land/masyaf", "color": "aqua"}
